[64.994297, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@851fbbe7f709:/# "]
[87.471443, "i", "grep -rn 'bplus_node' / --exclude-dir={proc,sys,usr,dev,lib,var,etc} 2>/dev/null\n"]
[87.471679, "o", "grep -rn 'bplus_node' / --exclude-dir={proc,sys,usr,dev,lib,var,etc} 2>/dev/null\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[87.476437, "o", "\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/trajectory.json\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K17\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K      \"message\": \"You are an AI assistant tasked with solving command-line tasks in a Linux environment. You will be given a task description and the output from previously executed commands. Your goal is to solve the task by providing batches of shell commands.\\n\\nFormat your response as JSON with the following structure:\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": \\\"Analyze the current state based on the terminal output provided. What do you see? What has been accomplished? What still needs to be done?\\\",\\n  \\\"plan\\\": \\\"Describe your plan for the next steps. What commands will you run and why? Be specific about what you expect each command to accomplish.\\\",\\n  \\\"commands\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"ls -la\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 0.1\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"cd project\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 0.1\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"task_complete\\\": true\\n}\\n\\nRequired fields:\\n- \\\"analysis\\\": Your analysis of the current situation\\n"]
[87.47669, "o", "- \\\"plan\\\": Your plan for the next steps\\n- \\\"commands\\\": Array of command objects to execute\\n\\nOptional fields:\\n- \\\"task_complete\\\": Boolean indicating if the task is complete (defaults to false if not present)\\n\\nCommand object structure:\\n- \\\"keystrokes\\\": String containing the exact keystrokes to send to the terminal (required)\\n- \\\"duration\\\": Number of seconds to wait for the command to complete before the next command will be executed (defaults to 1.0 if not present)\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The text inside \\\"keystrokes\\\" will be used completely verbatim as keystrokes. Write commands exactly as you want them sent to the terminal:\\n- You must end every command with a newline (\\\\n) or it will not execute.\\n- For special key sequences, use tmux-style escape sequences:\\n  - C-c for Ctrl+C\\n  - C-d for Ctrl+D\\n\\nThe \\\"duration\\\" attribute specifies the number of seconds to wait for the command to complete (default: 1.0) before the next command will be executed. On immediate tasks (e.g., cd, ls, echo, cat) set a dura"]
[87.476734, "o", "tion of 0.1 seconds. On commands (e.g., gcc, find, rustc) set a duration of 1.0 seconds. On slow commands (e.g., make, python3 [long running script], wget [file]) set an appropriate duration as you determine necessary.\\n\\nIt is better to set a smaller duration than a longer duration. It is always possible to wait again if the prior output has not finished, by running {\\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"duration\\\": 10.0} on subsequent requests to wait longer. Never wait longer than 60 seconds; prefer to poll to see intermediate result status.\\n\\nImportant notes:\\n- Each command's keystrokes are sent exactly as written to the terminal\\n- Do not include extra whitespace before or after the keystrokes unless it's part of the intended command\\n- Extra text before or after the JSON will generate warnings but be tolerated\\n- The JSON must be valid - use proper escaping for quotes and special characters within strings\\n- Commands array can be empty if you want to wait without taking action\\n\\nTask Description:\\n# Task: B+ Tree "]
[87.476741, "o", "Implementation\\n\\nYour goal is to implement a B+ Tree in C.\\n\\nIn the `target.c` file, you will find the `\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbplus_node\u001b[m\u001b[K` struct and the global `root` pointer. \\nYou must implement two functions: `bplus_insert` and `bplus_delete`.\\n\\n## Requirements\\nUnlike a standard B-Tree, a **B+ Tree** has strict structural requirements:\\n1. **Data at Leaves**: All data values must be stored strictly at the leaf level. Internal nodes only store keys for routing.\\n2. **Linked Leaves**: Every leaf node must maintain a `next` pointer to the adjacent leaf node, forming a linked list for fast range queries.\\n3. **Borrowing and Merging**: During deletion, if a node underflows, you must correctly borrow from a sibling or merge nodes while simultaneously keeping the leaf-level linked list completely intact.\\n\\nThe stress test will insert 10,000 nodes, delete 5,000 of them, and then verify that the B+ Tree properties hold, and critically, that traversing the leaf-level linked list yields a perfectly sorted array of th"]
[87.476748, "o", "e remaining 5,000 elements.\\n\\nWhen you are done, compile the code using `gcc -O2 -Wall -Werror -o /out/bplus_bench target.c` and ensure it runs successfully without crashing. If it prints \\\"FLAG: B+ Tree Success!\\\", you have passed.\\n\\n\\nCurrent terminal state:\\nCurrent Terminal Screen:\\nroot@851fbbe7f709:/#\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\"\r\n\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/trajectory.json\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K160\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K      \"message\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": \\\"No `.c` file was found using my restricted find command. Let me do a broad search of `.c` files, and also check if there is a `\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbplus_node\u001b[m\u001b[K` or `target.c` anywhere at all.\\\",\\n  \\\"plan\\\": \\\"I will list the contents of the `/out` directory, and run `find / -type f -name \\\\\\\"*.c\\\\\\\"` to locate any C files in the system.\\\",\\n  \\\"commands\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"ls -la /out/\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 0.1\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"find / -type f -name"]
[87.476753, "o", " \\\\\\\"*.c\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v \\\\\\\"/usr/\\\\|/sys/\\\\|/proc/\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 2.0\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\r\n"]
[87.477161, "o", "\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K153\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004hroot@851fbbe7f709:/# grep -rn '\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbplus_node\u001b[m\u001b[K' / --exclude-dir={proc,sys,usr,dev,lib,var,etc} 2>/dev/null\r\r\n"]
[87.47739, "o", "\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K154\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/trajectory.json\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K17\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K      \"message\": \"You are an AI assistant tasked with solving command-line tasks in a Linux environment. You will be given a task description and the output from previously executed commands. Your goal is to solve the task by providing batches of shell commands.\\n\\nFormat your response as JSON with the following structure:\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": \\\"Analyze the current state based on the terminal output provided. What do you see? What has been accomplished? What still needs to be done?\\\",\\n  \\\"plan\\\": \\\"Describe your plan for the next steps. What commands will you run and why? Be specific about what you expect each command to accomplish.\\\",\\n  \\\"commands\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"ls -la\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 0.1\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"cd project\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 0.1\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"task_"]
[87.477416, "o", "complete\\\": true\\n}\\n\\nRequired fields:\\n- \\\"analysis\\\": Your analysis of the current situation\\n- \\\"plan\\\": Your plan for the next steps\\n- \\\"commands\\\": Array of command objects to execute\\n\\nOptional fields:\\n- \\\"task_complete\\\": Boolean indicating if the task is complete (defaults to false if not present)\\n\\nCommand object structure:\\n- \\\"keystrokes\\\": String containing the exact keystrokes to send to the terminal (required)\\n- \\\"duration\\\": Number of seconds to wait for the command to complete before the next command will be executed (defaults to 1.0 if not present)\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The text inside \\\"keystrokes\\\" will be used completely verbatim as keystrokes. Write commands exactly as you want them sent to the terminal:\\n- You must end every command with a newline (\\\\n) or it will not execute.\\n- For special key sequences, use tmux-style escape sequences:\\n  - C-c for Ctrl+C\\n  - C-d for Ctrl+D\\n\\nThe \\\"duration\\\" attribute specifies the number of seconds to wait for the command to complete (default: 1.0) "]
[87.47742, "o", "before the next command will be executed. On immediate tasks (e.g., cd, ls, echo, cat) set a duration of 0.1 seconds. On commands (e.g., gcc, find, rustc) set a duration of 1.0 seconds. On slow commands (e.g., make, python3 [long running script], wget [file]) set an appropriate duration as you determine necessary.\\n\\nIt is better to set a smaller duration than a longer duration. It is always possible to wait again if the prior output has not finished, by running {\\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"duration\\\": 10.0} on subsequent requests to wait longer. Never wait longer than 60 seconds; prefer to poll to see intermediate result status.\\n\\nImportant notes:\\n- Each command's keystrokes are sent exactly as written to the terminal\\n- Do not include extra whitespace before or after the keystrokes unless it's part of the intended command\\n- Extra text before or after the JSON will generate warnings but be tolerated\\n- The JSON must be valid - use proper escaping for quotes and special characters within strings\\n- Commands ar"]
[87.477424, "o", "ray can be empty if you want to wait without taking action\\n\\nTask Description:\\n# Task: B+ Tree Implementation\\n\\nYour goal is to implement a B+ Tree in C.\\n\\nIn the `target.c` file, you will find the `\u001b[01;31m\u001b[K\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbplus_node\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[m\u001b[K` struct and the global `root` pointer. \\nYou must implement two functions: `bplus_insert` and `bplus_delete`.\\n\\n## Requirements\\nUnlike a standard B-Tree, a **B+ Tree** has strict structural requirements:\\n1. **Data at Leaves**: All data values must be stored strictly at the leaf level. Internal nodes only store keys for routing.\\n2. **Linked Leaves**: Every leaf node must maintain a `next` pointer to the adjacent leaf node, forming a linked list for fast range queries.\\n3. **Borrowing and Merging**: During deletion, if a node underflows, you must correctly borrow from a sibling or merge nodes while simultaneously keeping the leaf-level linked list completely intact.\\n\\nThe stress test will insert 10,000 nodes, delete 5,000 of them, and then verify that the B+ Tree"]
[87.477486, "o", " properties hold, and critically, that traversing the leaf-level linked list yields a perfectly sorted array of the remaining 5,000 elements.\\n\\nWhen you are done, compile the code using `gcc -O2 -Wall -Werror -o /out/bplus_bench target.c` and ensure it runs successfully without crashing. If it prints \\\"FLAG: B+ Tree Success!\\\", you have passed.\\n\\n\\nCurrent terminal state:\\nCurrent Terminal Screen:\\nroot@851fbbe7f709:/#\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\"\r\r\n\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K155\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[35m\u001b[K/logs/agent/trajectory.json\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32m\u001b[K160\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[36m\u001b[K:\u001b[m\u001b[K      \"message\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"analysis\\\": \\\"No `.c` file was found using my restricted find command. Let me do a broad search of `.c` files, and also check if there is a `\u001b[01;31m\u001b[K\u001b[01;31m\u001b[Kbplus_node\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[m\u001b[K` or `target.c` anywhere at all.\\\",\\n  \\\"plan\\\": \\\"I will list the contents of the `/out` directory, and run `find / -t"]
[87.477504, "o", "ype f -name \\\\\\\"*.c\\\\\\\"` to locate any C files in the system.\\\",\\n  \\\"commands\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"ls -la /out/\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 0.1\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"keystrokes\\\": \\\"find / -type f -name \\\\\\\"*.c\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | grep -v \\\\\\\"/usr/\\\\|/sys/\\\\|/proc/\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"duration\\\": 2.0\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\r\r\n"]
